= The Sheep in Wolf's Clothing

Bay İnönü's dining room was enveloped in smoke and marked by an atmosphere of tension. Amid a gathering of party bureaucrats, who ate and argued in black suits, <span class="wide_tooltip-wrap">Kırıkoğlu<span class="wide_tooltip-tip"><img src="img/portraits/KamilKirikoglu.jpg">Kamil Kırıkoğlu, the general secretary of the party since 1971. Even though a well-known Marxist, he was chosen by İnönü based on his personal tenets and acknowledged accessibility to appease the party left. However, now that the battle is between the old İnönü and the young Ecevit, he has to make a choice. Will he try to swim against the stream or try to lead it?</span></span> stood apart in his white suit and black coat.

He had never wished to attend this meal, nor to belong to this stagnant and forsaken party. The organization was dominated by elitist bureaucrats, disconnected from and indifferent to the genuine hardships faced by workers and the broader population. Nevertheless, circumstances had led him to the position of secretariat, not due to any affinity from İnönü or the other elites for his ideological views. Rather, he had attained this role through personal effort and discipline, motivated by a desire for the CHP to fulfill its historic mission of establishing genuine democracy by removing barriers to a mass Socialist party. He felt no concern for the future of the CHP; it could ultimately be consigned to history.

He considered the alternatives. Ecevit was not a viable option; he was a social democrat more focused on opposing Marxists than addressing the real issues confronting workers. Ecevit lacked the discipline necessary to manage such a large party. What troubled him most was that, despite Kırıkoğlu having introduced Ecevit to the party, it was Ecevit, not himself, whom fate had chosen to lead the opposition against İnönü.

Yet, what other choice did he have?

He was suddenly woken up from his thoughts with İnönü facing towards him.

İnönü’s left hand gently rose from the table as it gently gestured at him.

/ “So, Kamil, what do you think about the current affairs in our party as our secretary?”

Kırıkoğlu’s brows let loose as he steadied himself on his seat, his head straight and upward, unlike many young upstarts when they see the National Chief.

+ “Do you mean the conflict between you and Bülent, sir?”

İnönü quickly and subtly nodded in agreement as he did not wait before asking his question.

/ “Yes. Who do you think is going to win the congress?”

National Chief’s question rang, echoing in deathly silence across the room… Kırıkoğlu’s eye went low, lazily circling across the plate as he gathered his thoughts; in the next couple of seconds, he will declare his direct opposition to the elitist structure of this party, right against its mightiest face… With a short breath, he spoke.

+ “Sir… You have gathered the winds of history against yourself. You are going to lose the upcoming congress.”

İnönü’s right hand tightened into a fist beneath the table as it rested on his lap, his eyebrows, grown bushy with age, furrowed as he exhaled and spoke.

/ “Do you think so, Kamil? Is it my fault that Satır decided to push for a congress without my directives? Was the coup my fault also?”

There was no turning back for Kırıkoğlu now, he had drawn his sword against the dragon in his own den, he would not accept desperate last pleas of everything wrong with this party.

+ “Sir, you were the one who took Bülent out from Aladdin's Lamp with your moves. You can't ask me to put him back when it was your moves which led to his rise.”

İnönü’s question in return was released within a second, as the old man had grown impatient to hear his refusal…

/ “And who are you standing with, Kamil?”

+ “You have to be replaced for CHP to reach its historical objective, sir. I wish things could have been different...”

The National Chief rises from his seat, his left hand turned to a fist as he points at Kırıkoğlu with the other, his voice coarse with age yet filled with frustration and anger.

/ “Kamil! Are you going to make THIS man the prime minister!? This bandit!?”

- @kirikoglubye: But Kırıkoğlu did not answer. He walked out the old, wooden door and unleashed himself into the darkest night outside.

@kirikoglubye
on-arrival: kemalist_marxists_support = 1

